WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:02.702
-Our first guest tonight
is a talented actress

00:00:02.702 --> 00:00:05.371
you know from her work in
"Knives Out," "No Time to Die,"

00:00:05.371 --> 00:00:08.675
and "The Gray Man," she stars
as Marilyn Monroe in "Blonde,"

00:00:08.675 --> 00:00:11.077
which is in select theaters now
and premieres globally

00:00:11.077 --> 00:00:13.112
on Netflix September 28th.

00:00:13.112 --> 00:00:14.781
Let's take a look.

00:00:14.781 --> 00:00:16.482
-Don't abandon me.

00:00:16.482 --> 00:00:19.018
-She's coming.

00:00:19.018 --> 00:00:20.720
She's almost here.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:26.092
-[Sniffles] Please come.

00:00:26.092 --> 00:00:30.096 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:30.096 --> 00:00:32.732
[ Stops crying ]

00:00:32.732 --> 00:00:39.038 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:39.038 --> 00:00:45.378 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:45.378 --> 00:00:46.513
[ Laughing ]

00:00:46.513 --> 00:00:49.515
[ Crowd chanting "Marilyn" ]

00:00:49.515 --> 00:00:54.887 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:54.887 --> 00:00:57.924
Please welcome to the show
Ana de Armas, everyone.

00:00:57.924 --> 00:01:00.927
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:00.927 --> 00:01:06.532 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:06.532 --> 00:01:12.104 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:12.104 --> 00:01:13.972
Thank you so much
for being here.

00:01:13.972 --> 00:01:15.374
-Thank you for having me.

00:01:15.374 --> 00:01:18.044
-It is -- we were talking
backstage. I love this film.

00:01:18.044 --> 00:01:22.415
It is so -- it's an emotional
roller coaster to watch.

00:01:22.415 --> 00:01:23.883
-Yes.
-I can only imagine

00:01:23.883 --> 00:01:26.452
what it was like to have worked
on it for months and months.

00:01:26.452 --> 00:01:28.220
-Same.
-Yeah. Okay, good.

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:33.493
-Very emotional, very immersive,
but also a beautiful experience.

00:01:33.493 --> 00:01:34.761
-A gorgeous film.
-Thank you.

00:01:34.761 --> 00:01:36.763
And it premiered at
the Venice Film Festival.

00:01:36.763 --> 00:01:38.831
I've never been. I've heard
it's incredibly glamorous.

00:01:38.831 --> 00:01:40.499
-It was my first time.
-First time there.

00:01:40.499 --> 00:01:44.070
And this is even cooler --
it's the first time your mom,

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:45.204
who I know is based in Cuba,

00:01:45.204 --> 00:01:47.406
got to go
to one of your premieres.

00:01:47.406 --> 00:01:50.009
-First premiere in Venice.

00:01:50.009 --> 00:01:53.712
So that was pretty
overwhelming for her.

00:01:53.712 --> 00:01:56.783
And I was just, you know,
working a lot and doing press

00:01:56.783 --> 00:01:58.284
and checking on her
every once in a while,

00:01:58.284 --> 00:02:00.719
"Are you okay? Are you okay?"
And she would just start crying

00:02:00.719 --> 00:02:02.555
every single time.
And I was like,

00:02:02.555 --> 00:02:05.191
"I am not going to ask you
any more if you're okay."

00:02:05.191 --> 00:02:06.759
Just -- We'll talk about it
later.

00:02:06.759 --> 00:02:09.228
-Was she crying because
she wasn't okay, or --

00:02:09.228 --> 00:02:10.229
[ Laughter ]

00:02:10.229 --> 00:02:11.431
-She was more than okay.
-Okay, good.

00:02:11.431 --> 00:02:13.333
-Yeah.
-It was, I would imagine

00:02:13.333 --> 00:02:14.600
it was an incredibly glamorous
night.

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:17.270
The film was beautifully
received, as well.

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:18.671
-Yeah.
-Is your mom someone who gives

00:02:18.671 --> 00:02:21.607
you notes on performance ever?

00:02:21.607 --> 00:02:25.044
-Not really.
I mean, she's, you know --

00:02:25.044 --> 00:02:30.082
I remember the time I brought
"Knives Out" to my home in Cuba,

00:02:30.082 --> 00:02:31.951
and I did, like, this kind of,
like, screening

00:02:31.951 --> 00:02:36.122
for my family and friends,
and at the end of the movie,

00:02:36.122 --> 00:02:38.090
she was just like,
"Daniel was amazing.

00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:42.061
And Jamie Lee Curtis was great.
And this other guy was great."

00:02:42.061 --> 00:02:44.497
And I'm like, "You know,
I'm in the movie, too."

00:02:44.497 --> 00:02:47.433
She was complimenting
everyone else.

00:02:47.433 --> 00:02:49.736
She's just happy I got a job,
you know? Yeah.

00:02:49.736 --> 00:02:51.704
-And you got -- She's like,
"Everyone was great,

00:02:51.704 --> 00:02:54.040
and I'm assuming you got paid,
which good."

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:55.175
-Pretty much.

00:02:55.175 --> 00:02:58.210
-You grew up in Cuba,
some in Spain.

00:02:58.210 --> 00:03:02.381
I imagine even at those
locations, you were aware

00:03:02.381 --> 00:03:05.518
of who Marilyn Monroe was.
Yes?

00:03:05.518 --> 00:03:09.722
-Oh, yeah. Kind of, yeah.
We did have Marilyn's films

00:03:09.722 --> 00:03:12.158
on TV in Cuba.
And of course, in Spain,

00:03:12.158 --> 00:03:14.427
I -- you know,
after studying drama school,

00:03:14.427 --> 00:03:16.029
I started watching more films.

00:03:16.029 --> 00:03:19.432
So I was very --
I knew who Marilyn Monroe was

00:03:19.432 --> 00:03:23.503
and what she meant, but I didn't
know about Norma Jean,

00:03:23.503 --> 00:03:27.974
who was that woman, you know,
playing that character.

00:03:27.974 --> 00:03:30.443
-We were talking backstage
as well about the fact that,

00:03:30.443 --> 00:03:33.246
I will admit, when I first heard
you were playing the part,

00:03:33.246 --> 00:03:36.215
I did not think
that you looked like her.

00:03:36.215 --> 00:03:39.686
You felt the same way.
And yet on camera --

00:03:39.686 --> 00:03:43.056
-Trust me, no one ever told me
I look like Marilyn Monroe.

00:03:43.056 --> 00:03:44.957
-Ever.
-I heard you always described

00:03:44.957 --> 00:03:46.325
yourself to people like that.

00:03:46.325 --> 00:03:48.227
You would say, "Just so
you know when you see me,

00:03:48.227 --> 00:03:49.528
I look just
like Marilyn Monroe."

00:03:49.528 --> 00:03:52.798
-Yeah. Well, she used to be
brunette, so...

00:03:52.798 --> 00:03:54.634
-She did.
-...that we have in common.

00:03:54.634 --> 00:03:57.103
-But you -- obviously,
a transformation like that

00:03:57.103 --> 00:04:00.906
required, you know,
some additional things

00:04:00.906 --> 00:04:02.141
other than
just an acting performance,

00:04:02.141 --> 00:04:04.711
including you had
your eyebrows bleached.

00:04:04.711 --> 00:04:06.545
That sounds...
-Painful.

00:04:06.545 --> 00:04:09.282
-And?
And what's your report back?

00:04:09.282 --> 00:04:11.984
-Well, you know,
I love my eyebrows.

00:04:11.984 --> 00:04:13.953
You know, it's -- you know,
women know

00:04:13.953 --> 00:04:16.656
how important eyebrows are.

00:04:16.656 --> 00:04:18.490
So they had to bleach them,

00:04:18.490 --> 00:04:21.126
and not only that, like, shave
the middle of my eyebrows

00:04:21.126 --> 00:04:24.997
to make them more, you know,
separate them like she had.

00:04:24.997 --> 00:04:27.366
So, but my hair was brunette.

00:04:27.366 --> 00:04:30.236
So I just didn't leave home
for two months.

00:04:30.236 --> 00:04:31.570
It was terrifying.

00:04:31.570 --> 00:04:34.874
-Why didn't they wait
until closer to the the movie?

00:04:34.874 --> 00:04:37.109
Did they bleach them
two months before,

00:04:37.109 --> 00:04:39.511
or was it just
after filming each day?

00:04:39.511 --> 00:04:42.682
-Well, you need to do
camera tests, and, you know --

00:04:42.682 --> 00:04:43.950
-I don't understand
how movies work.

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:45.384
-The movie was for nine weeks.
Yeah.

00:04:45.384 --> 00:04:47.754
[ Laughter ]

00:04:47.754 --> 00:04:52.125
-Can they, like, edit and stuff?

00:04:52.125 --> 00:04:55.895
This is -- You might
not naturally make people

00:04:55.895 --> 00:04:59.832
think of Marilyn Monroe,
but you had someone in your life

00:04:59.832 --> 00:05:02.535
who was a perfect match
for someone in Marilyn's life.

00:05:02.535 --> 00:05:04.703
-Yes, I do.
-There's a very dramatic scene

00:05:04.703 --> 00:05:08.507
with a dog.
-Yes. Which is very --

00:05:08.507 --> 00:05:11.444
When I watch it, I don't know
how to feel because of course,

00:05:11.444 --> 00:05:14.880
it's a very serious,
important scene in the film.

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:16.815
And then at the same time,
I'm just looking at him

00:05:16.815 --> 00:05:20.252
and I'm melting.
-This the dog that --

00:05:20.252 --> 00:05:22.889
this is Marilyn's dog
right here.

00:05:22.889 --> 00:05:24.090
That's a photo of Marilyn's dog.

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:26.358
-Yeah.
-This is the dog in the film,

00:05:26.358 --> 00:05:28.561
and that's actually
your real-life dog.

00:05:28.561 --> 00:05:30.796
-That's Elvis. That's my dog.
Yeah.

00:05:30.796 --> 00:05:32.898
-So when did it occur
to everyone

00:05:32.898 --> 00:05:35.134
that you actually had
the perfect dog for the part?

00:05:35.134 --> 00:05:37.804
Did Elvis, like,
nose his headshot over one day?

00:05:37.804 --> 00:05:39.505
-[ Laughs ]

00:05:39.505 --> 00:05:42.308
Well, that's the dog --
Sinatra gave her that dog.

00:05:42.308 --> 00:05:44.376
-Okay.
-And the dog's name is Mafia.

00:05:44.376 --> 00:05:46.111
-Okay.
-Obviously.

00:05:46.111 --> 00:05:50.083
And we found this out,
and Andrew,

00:05:50.083 --> 00:05:51.950
as the great visionary
that he is --

00:05:51.950 --> 00:05:53.986
he saw
I look like Marilyn Monroe.

00:05:53.986 --> 00:05:57.189
Obviously, he saw that Elvis
was perfect for Mafia,

00:05:57.189 --> 00:05:59.392
and he was the one --
he had the idea, like,

00:05:59.392 --> 00:06:00.759
we need Elvis in the movie.

00:06:00.759 --> 00:06:04.163
-And now -- so again,
you're doing -- I would not.

00:06:04.163 --> 00:06:06.032
We had my dog here one time

00:06:06.032 --> 00:06:08.267
and I was stressed out
the whole day

00:06:08.267 --> 00:06:11.704
because -- well, I should note
my dog is dumb as rocks.

00:06:11.704 --> 00:06:16.875
And were you -- did it bring you
any stress to have people --

00:06:16.875 --> 00:06:18.777
an entire crew of people,

00:06:18.777 --> 00:06:20.813
you know,
basically relying on Elvis?

00:06:20.813 --> 00:06:23.616
Well -- Oh, well,
he was only in two scenes --

00:06:23.616 --> 00:06:25.585
this one that didn't make
the cut.

00:06:25.585 --> 00:06:27.987
-Oh, God. Did you have
to break it to him?

00:06:27.987 --> 00:06:30.022
[ Laughter ]

00:06:30.022 --> 00:06:32.224
-And then the other one
that's the most important one.

00:06:32.224 --> 00:06:34.760
But he's very well behaved.
-Yeah.

00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:36.962
-He's 13, so he's been
on sets with me.

00:06:36.962 --> 00:06:39.331
-And he seems to take
to stardom.

00:06:39.331 --> 00:06:42.868
-Clearly, he's very,
very spoiled.

00:06:42.868 --> 00:06:45.838
My makeup artist, Tina,
was making sure he was fresh

00:06:45.838 --> 00:06:47.606
and like...

00:06:47.606 --> 00:06:50.809
-And he wants to do a spin off,
is that right?

00:06:50.809 --> 00:06:53.312
He wants his...
-Yeah, no chance.

00:06:53.312 --> 00:06:54.680
-[Laughing] No chance.
-No chance.

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:57.082
What's funny about this is that
that chair was

00:06:57.082 --> 00:06:59.786
another actor's chair
that was doing a scene,

00:06:59.786 --> 00:07:02.021
and when he finished,
he came back,

00:07:02.021 --> 00:07:04.624
his backpack was on the floor,
and he was just like...

00:07:04.624 --> 00:07:05.757
[ Laughter ]

00:07:05.757 --> 00:07:08.094
Okay, I get it.

00:07:08.094 --> 00:07:11.063
-Yeah, because based
on that chair --

00:07:11.063 --> 00:07:12.197
Well, now I guess
that's what --

00:07:12.197 --> 00:07:14.133
Did Elvis get there up
on his own?

00:07:14.133 --> 00:07:15.134
Because I feel like...
-No,

00:07:15.134 --> 00:07:16.502
-Yeah, he needed help.
-Yeah.

00:07:16.502 --> 00:07:18.471
-So if I was the actor,
I wouldn't be like "Bad dog."

00:07:18.471 --> 00:07:20.940
I'd be like "Bad costars."
-Yes. [ Laughs ]

00:07:22.941 --> 00:07:25.410
-One of the things I enjoyed
the most -- and again,

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:27.647
you're wonderful in this film --
we are so used to seeing you

00:07:27.647 --> 00:07:30.983
in very fast-paced films
like "Gray Man"

00:07:30.983 --> 00:07:32.784
or "No Time to Die,"
you know even "Knives Out,"

00:07:32.784 --> 00:07:34.720
to some degree.
This is such a --

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:37.289
again, it's fascinating
to watch her entire life.

00:07:37.289 --> 00:07:38.857
-It's faced-paced
in a different way.

00:07:38.857 --> 00:07:40.559
-Yes, that's very right,
but it does --

00:07:40.559 --> 00:07:42.861
I mean, again, Andrew Dominik,
the director,

00:07:42.861 --> 00:07:46.499
I think he has such an eye
for taking his time

00:07:46.499 --> 00:07:48.267
and letting you
really feel the character.

00:07:48.267 --> 00:07:51.136
Did it feel like a more patient
performance

00:07:51.136 --> 00:07:52.905
while you were doing it or --

00:07:52.905 --> 00:07:55.407
because it feels very lived in,
I guess is what I'm saying?

00:07:55.407 --> 00:07:57.777
You don't seem to be in a rush
in a really nice way.

00:07:57.777 --> 00:08:00.012
-Oh, well,
that's not what happened.

00:08:00.012 --> 00:08:02.414
-Okay.
-I was actually pretty rushed.

00:08:02.414 --> 00:08:05.417
We didn't have money.
We didn't have time to do --

00:08:05.417 --> 00:08:07.453
to do the movie.
-Well, they had to use your dog.

00:08:07.453 --> 00:08:11.290
That's a dead giveaway.
-So, yeah.

00:08:11.290 --> 00:08:12.624
-They're just walking around
set,

00:08:12.624 --> 00:08:15.494
being like, "Anyone got a dog
that looks like this?"

00:08:15.494 --> 00:08:16.628
[ Laughter ]

00:08:16.628 --> 00:08:19.465
-Pretty much.
-That is fascinating to me

00:08:19.465 --> 00:08:22.868
to hear that you've got --
it is such a beautiful film.

00:08:22.868 --> 00:08:25.004
And you know, so,
credit to everyone involved

00:08:25.004 --> 00:08:26.672
because you would never
look at it and say,

00:08:26.672 --> 00:08:28.040
"Oh, they were definitely
cutting corners here."

00:08:28.040 --> 00:08:29.542
It's gorgeous.
-Yeah. Thank you.

00:08:29.542 --> 00:08:32.110
So, yeah,
Andrew is a fantastic director.

00:08:32.110 --> 00:08:34.079
He's very thoughtful
and very respectful,

00:08:34.079 --> 00:08:36.715
and he was very passionate
about this story,

00:08:36.715 --> 00:08:39.685
and he, you know --
he took it very seriously.

00:08:39.685 --> 00:08:44.657
He's very, you know,
detailed and perfectionist,

00:08:44.657 --> 00:08:47.259
and so am I, so...
-It was a perfect match.

00:08:47.259 --> 00:08:49.261
And it's a great film, and thank
you so much for being here.

00:08:49.261 --> 00:08:50.529
So lovely to meet you in person.
-Thank you so much.

00:08:50.529 --> 00:08:53.065
You guys, that's Ana de Armas.

00:08:53.065 --> 00:08:56.101
"Blonde" is in select theaters
now and premieres globally

00:08:56.101 --> 00:08:57.603
on Netflix September 28th.

00:08:57.603 --> 00:08:59.238
We'll be right back
with Reba McEntire.

